course: dictionary
lesson: 7811
title: Queue: a line of people or things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A queue is a line of people waiting for their turn, such as at a shop or a bus stop. To queue means to wait in that line. «In the UK people value queuing very highly, and jumping the queue makes them upset.» The words queuing and jumping tell you that a queue is a line where you join at the back and wait your turn.
explain: A queue can also be a list of things waiting in line, like songs or print jobs on a computer. The same idea of a line applies: new things join at the back, and the first one leaves at the front.
explain: Use the clues in the sentence. «Waiting your turn in a queue is fair because everyone's time counts the same, and pushing in steals other people's minutes.» Here queue means the line, and the sentence explains why the line matters. To check your meaning, swap in "line" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A queue is a line of people waiting their turn.
fact: To queue is to wait in a line.
fact: A queue can also be a list of things waiting, like songs or print jobs.
fact: «Jump the queue» means to push in ahead of others, which is unfair.
fact: Wild hermit crabs sometimes queue by size and swap shells one after another, each moving into the shell left by the next biggest.
exercise: «Waiting your turn in a queue is fair.» What does queue mean in this sentence? | A shop window | *A line of people waiting their turn | A type of food | A fast car
exercise: What does it mean to queue? | To pay for things at the till | *To wait in a line | To run very fast | To shout loudly
exercise: «Jump the queue» means what? | To skip school | *To push in ahead of others, which is unfair | To hop over a puddle | To wait patiently at the back
test: Where do new people join a queue? | At the front | In the middle of the road | *At the back | Halfway along
test: A queue of songs means what? | A song with lots of verses | *A list of songs waiting to be played | A choir singing together | A loud noise from the radio
exam: Which sentence uses queue to mean a list of things waiting, not people? | We stood in a queue for the bus | He jumped the queue at lunch | She will queue for tickets | *The print queue has five jobs left
